\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067055/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ebe52254f788487dd8b48fca8c88b8d691e0d3aacbe56d9ffaeacc744b22749"} {"question" "q-168498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067055/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ebe52254f788487dd8b48fca8c88b8d691e0d3aacbe56d9ffaeacc744b22749"} {"question" "q-168498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067055/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bfff0b6ed25340fe5a3b2429b31754c21e9b716e2dac0d4b7970f47ff3439fa"} {"question" "q-168498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067055/s