question" "q-324453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067055/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ebe52254f788487dd8b48fca8c88b8d691e0d3aacbe56d9ffaeacc744b22749"} {"question" "q-324453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067055/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bfff0b6ed25340fe5a3b2429b31754c21e9b716e2dac0d4b7970f47ff3439fa"} {"question" "q-324453" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Ac