}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R"] ["math" "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] ["math" "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] ["math" "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067055/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "0d8b61f5a87af9a8fbdc46dbfeff673ea0367b09fdd7f574e40e3f3bc85e1d98" "rationale" "For A=[[1,3],[1,-1]], A(-1,1)=−2(-1,1) and A(3,1)=2(3,1). These independent eigenvectors give the two modes shown in original option c (index 2), matching the authentic worked solution. Submitted option e omitted the negative-eigenvalue mode and was graded Incorrect; this is a derived source-backed judgment. Stored and captured solutions preserve the same complete family."}] "usage" {"e-5974" [] "e-5606" [] "q-39700" [] "q-324453" [] "e-5605" []} "basis" 2239 "retractions" [[17592186129689 :question/problem "Let $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 4$ and $λ_{2} = 0$, respectively.\n\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$?"] [17592186129689 :question/answer-fields 17592186127928] [17592186129522 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 3x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$.\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} =-2$ and $λ_{2} = 2$.*"] [17592186129522 :question/answer-fields 17592186126926] [17592186128917 :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=2$, respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$?"] [17592186128917 :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$, the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128889 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 6x_{1}(t) + 7x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) - 4x_{2}(t)$.\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=3$.*"] [17592186128889 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nfor the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A$.\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n$\\lambda_1=-1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lambda_2=3$.\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.For $\\lambda_1 =-1$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$For $\\lambda_2 =3$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThus, the general solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}v_{1}e^{λ_{1}t} + c_{2}v_{2}e^{λ_{2}t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128888 :question/problem "Find the substitution that will decouple the following system of differential equations:\n\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)$"] [17592186128888 :question/worked-solution "Let's construct the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nfor the given system:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A$.\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are\n$\\lambda_1=-3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lambda_2=1$.\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.For $\\lambda_1 =-3$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$For $\\lambda_2 =1$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we have $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = E\\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n{x_{1}(t) = 4u_{1}(t); x_{2}(t) = 3u_{1}(t) + u_{2}(t)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186126927 17592186126928 17592186126929 17592186126930 17592186126931 17592186127929 17592186127930 17592186127931 17592186127932 17592186127933] "immutable_fields" [[17592186126926 :answer-field/correct] [17592186126926 :answer-field/id] [17592186126926 :answer-field/key] [17592186126926 :answer-field/type] [17